¶
{{smaller block|As a ''thistle-downe'' in th' ayre doth flie,<br>So vainly shalt thou too and fro be tost.
{{right|''Spenser'', Mother Hub. Tale, l. 634.}}}}

{{smaller block|First loves were apt to float away from memory as ''thistledowns'' upon a summer breeze.
{{right|''The Century'', XL. 681.}}}}

'''thistle-finch''' (this′l-finch), ''n.'' One of several different fringilline birds which feed to a notable extent on the seeds of the thistle and various related composites. {{smaller|This name, or an equivalent, is traceable to the ἀκανθίδες of Aristotle (compare the extract given under ''thistlewarp'' below), and covers numerous species of}}

readings.c1s4:
{{smaller block|The land, once lean,
Or fertile only in its own disgrace,
Exults to see its ''thistly'' curse repeal'd.
{{right|''Cowper'', Task, vi. 768.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ground is ''thistly'', and not pleasurable to bare feet.
{{right|''Ruskin'', Elements of Drawing, p. 218.}}}}

2. Resembling a thistle or some attribute of a thistle; prickly.

{{smaller block|The rough Hedg-hog . . .
On 's ''thistly'' bristles rowles him quickly in.
{{right|''Sylvester'', tr. of Du Bartas's Weeks, i. 6.}}}}

{{smaller block|A beautiful Maltese [cat] with great yellow eyes, fur as soft as velvet, and silvery paws as lovely to look at as they were ''thistly'' to touch.
{{right|''R. T. Cooke'', Somebody's Neighbors, p. 48.}}}}

'''thiswise†''' (ᴛ̵ʜis′wīz), ''adv.'' [< ''this'' + ''-wise''.] In this manner; thus.

{{smaller block|Which text may ''thiswise'' be understood: that, as that sin shall be punished with everlasting damnation in the life to come, even so shall it not escape vengeance here.
{{right|''Tyndale'', Ans. to Sir T. More, etc. (Parker Soc., 1850), p. 24.}}}}

'''thithen†''', ''adv.'' See ''thethen''.

'''thither''' (ᴛ̵ʜiᴛ̵ʜ′ėr), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''thider'', ''thyder'', ''thydur'', ''thuder'', ''theder'', ''thedur'', ''thudere'', < AS. ''thider'', ''thyder'' = Icel. ''thadhra'', thither; cf. Goth. ''thathrō'', thence, then; < *''tha'', the pronominal base of ''the'', ''that'', etc., + ''-der'', a compar. suffix seen also in ''hither'', ''whither'', ''after'', ''yonder'', etc. Cf. Skt. ''tatra'', there, thither.] 1. To that place: opposed to ''hither''.

{{smaller block|Whan the kouherd com ''thid''[''er'']''e'' he koured lowe
To bi-hold in at the hole whi his hound berkyd.
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 47.}}}}

{{smaller block|Where I am, ''thither'' ye cannot come. {{right|John vii. 34.}}}}

2. To that point, degree, or result; to that end.

{{smaller block|This wrestler shall clear all: nothing remains but that}}

readings.c1s5:
{{smaller block|I kindle the boy ''thither.''
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, i. 1. 179.}}}}

'''Hither and thither.''' See ''hither.''

'''thither''' (ᴛ̵ʜiᴛ̵ʜ′ėr), ''a.'' [< ''thither'', ''adv.''] Being in that place or direction; hence, further;

readings.c3s2:
fer.] I. ''trans.'' 1. To bear; undergo; sustain; put up with; stand.

{{smaller block|Thei prechen that penaunce is profitable to the soule,
And what myschief and malese Cryst for man ''tholed.''
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xiii. 76.}}}}

{{smaller block|We've done nae ill, we'll ''thole'' nae wrang.
{{right|''Lads of Wamphray'' (Child's Ballads, VI. 172).}}}}

{{smaller block|Thou goest about a-sighing and a-moaning in a way that I can't stand or ''thole.'' {{right|''Mrs. Gaskell,'' Ruth, xvi.}}}}

2. To experience; feel; suffer.

{{smaller block|God, that ''tholede'' passiun,
The holde, sire, longe aliue.
{{right|''King Horn'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 67.}}}}

{{smaller block|So muche wo as I have with you ''tholed.''
{{right|''Chaucer,'' Friar's Tale, l. 248.}}}}

{{smaller block|The long reign of utter wretchedness, the nineteen winters which England had ''tholed'' for her sins.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman,'' Norman Conquest, V. 219.}}}}

3. To tolerate; permit; allow.

{{smaller block|I salle hys commandement holde, ȝif Criste wil me ''thole!''
{{right|''Morte Arthure'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 4151.}}}}

{{smaller block|Trewly he is on-lyue,
That ''tholede'' the Jewes his flessh to riffe,
He lete vs fele his woundes fyue,
Oure lorde verray.
{{right|''York Plays,'' p. 453.}}}}

4. To admit of; afford.

{{smaller block|He gaed to his gude wife
Wi' a' the speed that he coud ''thole.''
{{right|''Lochmaben Harper'' (Child's Ballads, VI. 3).}}}}

5. To give freely. ''Halliwell.''

II. ''intrans.'' 1. To endure grief, pain, misfortune, etc.; suffer.

{{smaller block|Manne on molde, be meke to me,}}
